version of the individual records. And so we're able to check within that six months, though

58:28.020 --> 58:34.500
because they have that six month rule on their side, if a crime older than six months is amended,

58:34.500 --> 58:40.580
I don't get it, right? And it's not in our crime site. And it's for that reason that when we calculate

58:40.580 --> 58:45.620
our long term statistics for the rankings, we use a six month period because it's only within

58:45.620 --> 58:50.740
that six months that I can, you know, it's probably a very small number that change, but I don't know.

58:50.740 --> 58:55.540
And since I don't know, I'm not going to pretend I do, right? And so that's why we worked on that

58:55.540 --> 59:00.820
thing. Now the sheriff's department, though they don't have the reputation, do a smarter thing.

59:00.820 --> 59:06.900
When they, when they give me the daily slice, they give me all amendments in the last six months,

59:06.900 --> 59:13.220
in the last six months. So rather than filtering on the crime date, they're filtering on the edited

59:13.540 --> 59:18.980
date, that their database keeps every time there's a change. And so that allows me to be more,

59:18.980 --> 59:24.980
you know, certain that you're getting it. But in my code, I have to make sure that I do all those

59:24.980 --> 59:28.900
changes, otherwise my data is going to get out of date and be wrong. There's also a process for

59:29.780 --> 59:34.740
removing crimes that have been deleted. And if a crime has been deleted, I check it in the database,

59:34.740 --> 59:40.180
we keep the record, we actually keep it on the map, and you can click on it and it'll say deleted.

59:40.180 --> 59:44.580
And then we'll explain to you that the police have deleted this crime, but we will omit it from the

59:44.580 --> 59:49.620
statistics, right? So when we calculate the numbers, we won't count it, but we'll still show it to you

59:49.620 --> 59:54.340
so you don't like come to our site one day, see a crime and come back and then it's gone and think

59:54.340 --> 59:58.340
that we're playing some game. You know what I mean? We're just going to be transparent about

59:58.340 --> 01:00:01.460
it got deleted. And that's a real pain in the butt, you have to write a lot of code.

01:00:01.940 --> 01:00:03.380
But it's worth doing.

01:00:10.500 --> 01:00:16.820
Hi, do you have like a system to test the accuracy of the information that you receive,

01:00:16.820 --> 01:00:21.860
even if it's from a government agency? How do you know to trust that that information

01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:25.780
is accurate? Sort of broadly as opposed to just the crime data?

01:00:26.580 --> 01:00:33.380
Yeah, so like yeah, so broadly, there's a couple, you know, things you can do that are just kind of,

01:00:33.380 --> 01:00:37.540
you know, they're not even really tricks, it's just like basic stuff that, you know,

01:00:37.540 --> 01:00:42.260
people call integrity tests or whatever. To me, the most basic thing is if you're getting line by

01:00:42.260 --> 01:00:48.340
line data that's provided to you by a source and they themselves publish some total somewhere

01:00:48.340 --> 01:00:54.100
or add it up somewhere, can you recreate the total that they've made? And I almost, if I can find

01:00:54.740 --> 01:01:00.260
that, that will always be my first goal is to say, can I recreate the analysis

01:01:00.260 --> 01:01:03.620
that they themselves have done? It doesn't mean their analysis is right,

01:01:03.620 --> 01:01:07.540
you know what I mean? But it means you understand how they do what they do and how they get there.

01:01:07.540 --> 01:01:12.020
And then if it does match, you know, you got a pretty good sense you're on track with them.

01:01:12.020 --> 01:01:14.900
And if it doesn't match, you get to ask some really interesting questions.

01:01:14.900 --> 01:01:18.340
Why is this? And then you can, and then that process you'll learn a lot, you know,

01:01:18.340 --> 01:01:22.500
and there's usually only a small number of people in the government, you know, agency that

01:01:22.500 --> 01:01:26.420
actually know how that happens. And once you get to talk to one of them, you can ask them

01:01:26.420 --> 01:01:30.180
all kinds of questions, you can learn all kinds of stuff, and you're able to kind of get some more.

01:01:30.180 --> 01:01:34.980
So to me, it's just like adding it up. It's, you know, even like just the total number of rows,

01:01:34.980 --> 01:01:37.940
you know what I mean? It's not like you're doing anything fancy. Like, do I have the same

01:01:38.740 --> 01:01:43.300
number of rows that they do? If they're omitting things, which people often are,

01:01:43.300 --> 01:01:46.660
for different reasons, omitting things from analysis, you know, you got to figure out

01:01:46.660 --> 01:01:50.820
what those are, right? It's like what I discovered looking at the fire department database and a

01:01:50.820 --> 01:01:54.980
lot of their problems, is they were omitting different things at different times, and they didn't

01:01:54.980 --> 01:01:59.460
know why, because different people were doing it, and it wasn't standardized, and we were really

01:01:59.460 --> 01:02:03.620
pressing them on it, writing all these stories, and then I got one of the memos that they wrote

01:02:03.620 --> 01:02:08.020
in that time period that we were pressuring them, and the memo about how to use the database said,

01:02:08.660 --> 01:02:12.820
what do we exclude? Question mark, you know what I mean? And so don't always assume that there's

01:02:12.820 --> 01:02:17.380
really like a system. You know, the people with the government generate this data, they're people

01:02:17.460 --> 01:02:21.060
just like you, they have, you know, often deadlines just like you, and people clujen

01:02:21.060 --> 01:02:24.980
stuff, you know what I mean? Numbers get clujes sometimes, particularly when it's not,

01:02:24.980 --> 01:02:29.220
though Argentina may be an exception, particularly when it's not like the really high level

01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:34.900
economic data that everyone is very closely watching, right? But, um, does that answer your

01:02:35.220 --> 01:02:40.260
question? Yeah, thank you. Okay. ¿Alguna otra? Dejamos acá.

01:02:45.780 --> 01:02:54.260
Do you guys write about what works, what are the best practices when a place where the crime goes

01:02:54.260 --> 01:03:01.380
down in a place where you reported high crime rates before? Sort of a good news kind of story,

01:03:01.460 --> 01:03:06.820
like on what works. Um, you know, probably not as much as we should, you know what I mean?

01:03:06.820 --> 01:03:11.780
The reality about crime, and this is probably my opinion, but you know, crime in the United

01:03:11.780 --> 01:03:17.300
States is going down over a really long term period, according to official crime reports,

01:03:17.300 --> 01:03:22.020
you know what I mean? Like way down, you know what I mean? You saw that story with 300 homicides,

01:03:22.020 --> 01:03:27.140
it was over a thousand like in the 90s. And so that's the big long term story about crime data

01:03:27.220 --> 01:03:31.860
in the United States. And I think that, that the media in the U.S. covers that a lot because

01:03:31.860 --> 01:03:37.060
politicians talk about it a lot, police chiefs talk about it a lot. It is remarkable. And that

01:03:37.060 --> 01:03:43.220
kind of big picture, like good news story about crime going down is very, very commonly told in

01:03:43.220 --> 01:03:52.020
the U.S. media. However, why? Right? Like, why is the crime going down is a debated thing? And that,

01:03:52.020 --> 01:03:55.140
you know, the mayor is going to tell you, it's because he and the police chief are such great

01:03:55.140 --> 01:03:59.300
mayors and police chiefs, right? And they're, they've reformed the police department to be more

01:03:59.300 --> 01:04:05.220
enlightened and da, da, da, da. Well, okay, that may be true, but major crime is going down in every

01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:09.380
city in the United States, and you're not mayor everywhere. And that mayor in that other town,

01:04:09.380 --> 01:04:15.940
he's in jail, so I mean, he wasn't a very good mayor, right? So there's some structural things

01:04:16.740 --> 01:04:22.260
happening in the, in at least the U.S., they're deeper, you know what I mean, than like one mayor

01:04:22.340 --> 01:04:27.140
or one approach to policing that are happening. And, and a lot of different ways we try to get at

01:04:27.140 --> 01:04:33.860
that, we've used our data to do a couple stories, like about, like areas within high crime neighborhoods

01:04:33.860 --> 01:04:38.100
that have lots of crimes, smaller, like sub neighborhood, like block, you know, areas that have

01:04:38.100 --> 01:04:42.500
like next to none, and like, why is that? And we've gone and done stories about a couple places

01:04:42.500 --> 01:04:46.820
like that to see what, what is that place like that leads to that happening, right? And there's

01:04:46.900 --> 01:04:52.820
some reasons, but I think that the, the really big ticket question in the U.S. about why crime is

01:04:52.820 --> 01:04:58.900
going down is still to be answered, and maybe can't be answered, you know, via some mix of economics

01:04:58.900 --> 01:05:03.540
and sociology and politics that kind of leads to it all. There's one person who wrote a controversial

01:05:03.540 --> 01:05:08.740
magazine article a few months ago claiming it's the reduction in lead and paint. There's less

01:05:08.740 --> 01:05:14.020
lead paint in, in like, in poor, in poor neighborhoods, and so fewer people have lead poisoning,

01:05:14.020 --> 01:05:17.460
and lead poisoning leads to all this other stuff, you know what I mean? And that's a theory,

01:05:18.020 --> 01:05:21.780
but how do you test it? How do we ever sort this out? I don't know if we'll really ever know the

01:05:21.780 --> 01:05:25.940
answer. Thank you, Ann.
